<body>
<header class="site-header"><div class="wrapper navbar"><a class="wordmark" href="#top">Baojuan Wang</a><nav aria-label="Primary navigation" class="navigation"><a href="#about">About</a><a href="#research">Research</a><a href="#publications">Publications</a><a href="#awards">Awards</a></nav></div></header>
<main class="wrapper main" id="top">
<section class="hero" id="about">
<div class="intro"><h1 class="hero-title">Baojuan<br/>Wang</h1><p class="name-cn">王宝娟</p><p class="position">Ph.D. student in Computer Science<br/>Drexel University</p><div class="quick-links"><a href="mailto:bw689@drexel.edu">Email</a><a href="https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=GTa0UdgAAAAJ&amp;hl=en" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Google Scholar</a><a href="https://orcid.org/0009-0008-6629-8993" rel="noopener" target="_blank">ORCID</a><a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/baojuan-wang/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">LinkedIn</a></div></div>
<div class="bio"><p>I am a Ph.D. student in Computer Science at <a href="https://drexel.edu/">Drexel University</a>, where I am a member of <a href="https://www.sepalab.com/">SePAL</a> and am advised by <a href="https://zhibosun.com/">Dr. Zhibo Eric Sun</a> and co-advised by <a href="https://yue.zyueinfosec.com/">Dr. Yue Zhang</a>. Before joining Drexel, I earned my M.S. in Computer Science from <a href="https://www.cqut.edu.cn/">Chongqing University of Technology</a>, advised by <a href="https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=qysA0NoAAAAJ&amp;hl=en">Dr. Wanping Liu</a>. I earned B.S. degrees in Statistics and Computer Science from <a href="https://www.ytu.edu.cn/">Yantai University</a>.</p><p>I study how people understand security information and decide what to do with it. I am also interested in how LLMs affect learning about software vulnerabilities and how they can be used to investigate online scams.</p></div>
</section>
<section class="research" id="research"><h2 class="section-title">Research</h2>
<article class="work"><div class="work-content"><div class="work-meta"><p class="publication-year">ACM CCS 2026</p><h3 class="work-title">Learning Less While Asking More: How LLM-Based Assistance Shapes Vulnerability Learning</h3><p class="author-line"><strong>Baojuan Wang</strong>, Yifan Yao, Yue Zhang, Zhibo Eric Sun</p></div><div class="work-summary"><p class="work-desc">We compared LLM chat and web search as tools for learning about software vulnerabilities in a study of 70 participants. Although participants rated the LLM as more helpful and clearer, web search was associated with larger gains in understanding vulnerability causes, runtime behavior, and security risks.</p><div class="linkline"><a href="https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3830454.3846814" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Paper</a></div></div></div></article>
<article class="work work--reverse"><div class="work-content"><div class="work-meta"><p class="publication-year">IEEE/ACM IWQoS 2026</p><h3 class="work-title">The Imitation Game: Using Large Language Models to Disrupt Chinese Chat-Based Cybercrime</h3><p class="author-line">Yifan Yao, <strong>Baojuan Wang</strong>, Jinhao Duan, Kaidi Xu, Chuankai Guo, Zhibo Eric Sun, Yue Zhang</p></div><div class="work-summary"><p class="work-desc">We wanted to see whether an LLM could hold a natural conversation with accounts promoting illicit services. Using LURE, we engaged 53 providers found across 98 Telegram groups. Thirty conversations continued naturally and revealed payment information, sometimes shared as images. Researchers reviewed each message before it was sent.</p><div class="linkline"><a href="https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1109/IWQoS70441.2026.11661137" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Paper</a></div></div></div></article>
</section>
<section class="publications" id="publications"><h2 class="section-title">Publications</h2><ol class="paper-list"><li><span class="year">2026</span><div><p class="ptitle">Learning Less While Asking More: How LLM-Based Assistance Shapes Vulnerability Learning</p><p class="pdetail"><strong>Baojuan Wang</strong>, Yifan Yao, Yue Zhang, Zhibo Eric Sun. <em>ACM CCS 2026.</em> <a href="https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3830454.3846814" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Paper</a></p></div></li><li><span class="year">2026</span><div><p class="ptitle">The Imitation Game: Using Large Language Models to Disrupt Chinese Chat-Based Cybercrime</p><p class="pdetail">Yifan Yao, <strong>Baojuan Wang</strong>, Jinhao Duan, Kaidi Xu, Chuankai Guo, Zhibo Eric Sun, Yue Zhang. <em>IEEE/ACM IWQoS 2026.</em> <a href="https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1109/IWQoS70441.2026.11661137" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Paper</a></p></div></li><li><span class="year">2024</span><div><p class="ptitle">CFOA: Exploring Transferable Adversarial Examples by Content Feature Optimization with Attention Guidance</p><p class="pdetail">Wanping Liu, <strong>Baojuan Wang</strong>, Dong Huang, Haolan Luo, Ling Lu. <em>Computers &amp; Security, 142, 103882.</em> <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0167404824001834" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Paper</a></p></div></li></ol></section>
<section class="additional-section" id="experience"><h2 class="section-title">Academic Experience</h2>
<div class="info-list">
<div class="info-row"><div><p class="info-role">Teaching Assistant · Drexel University</p><p class="info-note">Introduction to Computer Science; Computer Programming I</p></div><span class="info-date">Sep 2026 – present</span></div>
<div class="info-row"><div><p class="info-role">Research Assistant · Drexel University</p></div><span class="info-date">Sep 2024 – Aug 2026</span></div>
